standard input, stdin signal and it is represented 0
is a signal through shell accepts the input i.e.. commands

stdout : signal which shell outputs the command 

#stdin (0) stream 0, signal value 0
#stdout (1) or stream1. signal value 1
#stderror = standard error signal (2), signal value 2  ,like invalid commands

echo "it is cold today!"  #signal 0
# it is cold today  #signal 1


echo "it is cold today!" > file.txt
# the output will be redirected to file.txt
# file.txt stores the signal 1 data

#to store the error in the command in a file use 2>
necho "Hello" 2> error.txt

cat error.txt
# bash: necho: command not found

# 2> redirect only errors to file, if the command works success then it will not store the output of the commands
# 2 is stderror signal

echo "Hello" 2> error.txt
# Hello

cat error.txt  #it is empty

# 1 is stdout signal, it will only rediects output of the command, not errors
# by default you can also >, instaed 1>
echo "Hello" 1> file.txt
cat file.txt
# Hello

# > will redirect the output to file and it will erase old content and add new output
# >> to append the new output use >> to redirect

echo "Hi" >> file.txt

-----

noclobber is a shell option that prevents you from accidentally overwriting (clobbering) an existing file when using the > redirection operator.

# bash: filename: cannot overwrite existing file.

# Turn it ON
set -o noclobber

echo "New Data" > notes.txt
# bash: notes.txt: cannot overwrite existing file

# Turn it OFF
set +o noclobber

-----

to redirect output or error to a file depending upon the situation, you use 2>&1

echo "Hi" output.txt 2>&1
cat output.txt
# Hi

eecho "Hi" output.txt 2>&1
cat output.txt
# bash: eecho: command not found

# /dev/null is a special virtual device file known as the "null device" or "bit bucket" that instantly discards any data written to it.
# Think of it as a digital black hole or a paper shredder. Anything you send into /dev/null disappears forever, and the system reports back that the write operation was successful. Additionally, if you try to read from /dev/null, it instantly returns an End-of-File (EOF) marker, meaning it acts as a completely empty file.



